{#
  ABOUT ME, THE CONTENT LAYER — Figma 664:61547 (resting) and 666:61738 (arrived).

  THIS PARTIAL IS NO LONGER A SECTION. It used to own a <section>, a sticky
  stage, the photo and a runway; all four now live in partials/story.njk, which
  pins ONE image band for the whole About -> Testimonials sequence. What is left
  here is exactly what rides over that band during beats A and B: the heading,
  the three paragraphs and the clock.

  WHY THE MERGE. As two sections they could not help but show the seam — a 60px
  band of the second section's background between the two photos, and side
  margins that flipped from white to black at the join. One section, one band,
  one scope: nothing to align and nothing to gap.

  IT STILL READS --about-progress AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. story.njk writes one
  property, --story-progress, over the whole sequence; _story.css derives
  --about-progress from its first 678.86px. So every rule in _about.css — the
  dim, the six colour ramps, the settle translate — is untouched and beat A is
  the committed behaviour to the bit.

  THE CLOCK IS THE EXISTING COMPONENT, MOVED AND NOT REBUILT. Every data-clock-*
  hook below is byte-identical to what scripts/clock.js binds, so that file has
  never needed an edit: the live Amsterdam time, the three rotating hands and the
  time-of-day status schedule all keep working. The comp's "01:51 / Probably in
  deep work" is one moment of that schedule, not a string to hardcode.

  ONE THING IS NEW IN THIS MARKUP: the "At" label before the time. 666:61759
  draws it as its own text node, Body Medium against the time's Title Large, so
  it is a span here rather than something CSS could fake.

  #about-me AND data-header-theme MOVED UP to the .story section, which is what
  the header reads and what analytics-events.js observes for view_about. They
  belong on the element that spans the whole sequence, not on this fragment.
#}

  <div class="about__summary">
    <div class="about__inner">

      <div class="about__content">
        <h2 class="about__heading">
          <span class="about__heading-lead">{{ about.heading.lead }}</span>
          <span class="about__heading-rest">{{ about.heading.rest }}</span>
        </h2>

        {#- RUNS, NOT A STRING. about.bio is a list of lists, so the emphasis the
            node specifies lives in the data. The whitespace control is
            load-bearing: the runs carry their own spaces and must be joined with
            nothing between them. The bold runs are what go white first. -#}
        {%- for para in about.bio %}
          <p class="about__bio">
            {%- for run in para -%}
              {%- if run.bold %}<strong>{{ run.text }}</strong>{% else %}{{ run.text }}{% endif -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
          </p>
        {%- endfor %}
      </div>

      <div class="about__clock" data-clock>
        <div class="about__clock-face">
          <svg
            class="about__clock-hands"
            viewBox="-22.3 -3.9 118 105.8"
            fill="none"
            aria-hidden="true"
            focusable="false"
          >
            {#- THE VIEWBOX IS OFFSET, NOT REDRAWN. clock.js pins PIVOT = "52 30"
                and rotates the hands about it, so those coordinates cannot move.
                Shifting the window instead (-22.3, -3.9) puts that same pivot at
                63% across and 32% down of the 90.5 x 81.14 box, which is where
                666:61719 has the hub, and 118 x 105.8 matches the box's aspect so
                nothing letterboxes. -#}
            <defs>
              {#- Along each hand's own length, dark at the hub and light at the
                  tip. userSpaceOnUse so the gradient rotates WITH the hand — a
                  transform on the line carries its paint with it. Two of them
                  because the hands are different lengths and one shared ramp
                  would leave the shorter hand's tip mid-gradient. -#}
              <linearGradient id="about-clock-hour" x1="52" y1="30" x2="52" y2="-9"
                              gradientUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
                <stop offset="0" stop-color="#8A8A8A" />
                <stop offset="1" stop-color="#DADADA" />
              </linearGradient>
              <linearGradient id="about-clock-minute" x1="52" y1="30" x2="52" y2="-17"
                              gradientUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
                <stop offset="0" stop-color="#8A8A8A" />
                <stop offset="1" stop-color="#DADADA" />
              </linearGradient>
            </defs>

            <line class="about__clock-hand about__clock-hand--hour" data-clock-hour
                  x1="52" y1="30" x2="52" y2="-9" />
            <line class="about__clock-hand about__clock-hand--minute" data-clock-minute
                  x1="52" y1="30" x2="52" y2="-17" />
            {#- The only hand that crosses the pivot: it runs a little past the hub
                on the far side, as 666:61719 draws it. -#}
            <line class="about__clock-hand about__clock-hand--second" data-clock-second
                  x1="52" y1="43" x2="52" y2="-33" />
            <circle class="about__clock-hub" cx="52" cy="30" r="6.5" />
          </svg>
          <p class="about__clock-time">
            <span class="about__clock-at">{{ about.clock.at }}</span>
            <span class="about__clock-value" data-clock-time>{{ about.clock.time }}</span>
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="about__clock-body">
        <p class="about__clock-status">
          <span class="about__clock-lead" data-clock-lead>{{ about.clock.statusLead }}</span>
          <span class="about__clock-headline" data-clock-status>{{ about.clock.status }}</span>
        </p>
        <p class="about__clock-detail" data-clock-detail>{{ about.clock.detail }}</p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>
